translation:
A goat to the herd,  
take the kid to the pen,  
make hay from there,  
I will give hay to the cow;  
the cow gives me milk,  
I give milk to the piglet,  
the piglet gives me a flank,  
I give the flank to the mother-in-law;  
the mother-in-law gives me cake,  
I give the cake to Kalev,  
Kalev gives me iron,  
I give the iron to the rack;  
the rack gives me gold,  
I give the gold to the oak;  
the oak gives me a leaf,  
I give the leaf to the lamb;  
the lamb gives me wool,  
I give the wool to Eerik;  
Eerik makes me a new hut.

Who adorns the alder forest?  
The schoolmaster adorns the alder forest;  
Whose meadows are lush?  
The grandfather's meadows are lush;  
Whose trees are red?  
Kristjan's trees are red;  
Whose yard is grassy?  
Toss's yard is grassy;  
Whose gates are brass?  
The blacksmith's gates are brass;  
Who has a hollow hill?  
At Kullamägi.

Let the brewers be healthy,
brewers, rock brewers,
makers of the vine of wrath.
Here is what was put together from six.
The first was water, which is liquid;
the second came strong and full;
the third - dear junipers;
the fourth - malt, which is sweet;
the fifth - sour hops,
the sixth - those hot stones,
then for the seventh yeast was added.
Thus the beer was made ready.

A little instrument, a little joy;  
Dear kannel, a little plaything;  
Without the instrument, no household;  
Without the kannel, no companion.  
The instrument walks to taverns’ paths,  
The kannel to beer houses.  
But it’s looking for vodka buyers,  
Calling out to vodka sellers.

The party is ending, the party is ending, little family elder;
Beer is finished, kvass is gone in the family room;
Beer is finished, yeast at the bottom –
The joyful party is over.

Hello father, hello mother,  
Hello master and mistress;  
Hello father-in-law, hello mother-in-law,  
Hello bride, my young maiden;  
Hello room with written box,  
Roof with written beams;  
Hello floor of paper,  
Hello courtyard of silver;  
Hello daughter with golden writing,  
Hello son with silver feet;  
Hello tin streets,  
Hello copper gates;  
Hello silver courtyard birds,  
Hello golden cuckoo cakes;  
Hello everyone throughout,  
All the household to the end.

Do not curse the maidens,
Swear on your pure soul;
Do not curse the maiden’s mother;
Swear on the old woman;
Who made a bride expensive,
A maiden for forty [units of currency]? -
Before, you got a wife without money:
You got a girl on the other side,
Now you cannot get a wife for money.
You get a girl from the other side;
A maiden’s hair costs a thousand,
A maiden’s braid costs a hundred,
Fifty for the linen necklace, - -

The power of toil, the power of labor;  
The power of sorrow, the power of tears;  
The power of austerity, the power of gloom.  
Oh, this dear power of Kapo!  
And this good power of Härvitada,  
Love Aruküla:  
There, thighs are beaten on the instrument,  
At the dance, stomped impatiently;  
Walls are struck with song!  
In austerity, beaten with a whip;  
With a slap, walls are struck.

There is a saber dog,  
without a master’s command,  
eats my fatty meat,  
sucks lard from the butt,  
from the sail eats soap fat,  
from the side gets chimney soot fat,  
from behind the hand gets rat fat,  
from the groin gets cake fat.

Mother Ella, dear granny!  
Heat the stove with kitchen wood;  
Honor the whip to Virve ella,  
I will take the whip to Virve;  
So the broom flies!  
Whip strikes on the back of the neck –  
Whack strikes on the neck;  
Blue marks on the back. – –  
Oh, poor Esna parish, –  
Among other parishes!  
In the middle of the district;  
Whip lashes heard from Laiuse;  
Whip lashes to Paide! – –  
Misery in Alutaguse! –  
Maid’s crying in the meadow;  
I cried before, I cried yesterday,  
Again I cry, today I cry. – –  
***  
Mill’s golden eyebrows have withered,  
The eyes have hardened and grown straight? –  
Into the harsh village;  
To the graveyard of Esna parish.
